# 68. Artemis Cognitive Failure and Degraded-Mode Rules

Artemis must degrade safely when a cognitive dependency fails.

## 68.1 Model Failure

If a selected model is unavailable, rate-limited, invalid or times out:

- Record the actual failure
- Apply an approved fallback only when allowed
- Preserve fallback provenance
- Do not fabricate a response
- Do not convert model absence into agreement
- Do not weaken required safety gates

If the workflow is safe with deterministic logic and internal evidence alone,
Artemis may proceed in a documented degraded mode.

If the model or verification capability is required by policy, Artemis must
return blocked or insufficient evidence.

## 68.2 Invalid Structured Output

Malformed or schema-invalid model/advisor output must not enter canonical
synthesis as valid evidence.

The system may perform a bounded repair/retry according to approved policy.

Repeated repair must not become unbounded token, latency or cost consumption.

## 68.3 Conflicting Cognitive Outputs

When multiple models or reasoning passes conflict:

- Preserve the conflict
- Check context compatibility
- Check source and model correlation
- Apply task-specific evaluation history where valid
- Allow Artemis to abstain

Do not force consensus solely to produce an answer.

## 68.4 Cognitive Timeout

A cognitive workflow must have bounded time and resource budgets.

If the deadline expires:

- Cancel unnecessary outstanding advisor work where supported
- Preserve completed valid inputs
- Mark missing inputs accurately
- Return a degraded, blocked or insufficient state according to policy

Do not allow unbounded deliberation to delay time-sensitive safety checks.

## 68.5 Deterministic Gate Precedence

A cognitive dependency failure must never cause a deterministic safety gate to
fail open.

When safety state is uncertain, execution remains blocked.

