# 69. Artemis Cognitive Architecture Completion Gate

Sections 60–68 are part of the Artemis architecture and refine Sections 45–58.
They do not authorize implementation by themselves.

The Dynamic Active-Work Rule remains the only implementation authority.

Before Artemis Core Stage 6 (`Real Artemis Evidence Orchestration`) may be
considered complete, the approved implementation must have a verified mapping
for:

- Canonical Decision Context ownership
- Cognitive Kernel ownership
- Deterministic versus model-assisted boundaries
- Model/advisor routing boundary
- Memory-to-cognition boundary
- Canonical Artemis Decision artifact
- Decision lineage
- Explicit cognitive abstention/failure states
- Cognitive Decision versus Execution Authorization semantics

Before Artemis Core Stage 15 (`Artemis Core Completion and Freeze`) may be
declared complete, the approved maturity level must additionally verify:

- Artemis identity is not coupled to one LLM/provider
- A raw model answer cannot become a canonical Artemis Decision directly
- Deterministic safety rules cannot be overridden by model output
- Canonical decisions are reconstructable
- Correlation and missing-evidence handling are real
- Memory cannot recursively turn Artemis opinions into independent evidence
- Model/advisor failures degrade safely
- Cognitive evaluation is operational for the declared scope
- User-facing explanations remain faithful to canonical decisions
- Required Human QA has passed
- Runtime provenance is exact

## 69.1 Architectural Test

A proposed Artemis implementation fails this architecture if its effective
logic can be reduced to:

`prompt -> one LLM -> answer -> execution`

A compliant Artemis architecture must preserve the separation of:

Context
→ Evidence
→ Deterministic validation/policy
→ Cognitive reasoning
→ Memory/advisory inputs where useful
→ Artemis synthesis
→ Canonical Artemis Decision
→ Independent control chain
→ Execution only when separately authorized

## 69.2 Final Cognitive Operating Principle

For cognition, the goal is not maximum model usage.

The goal is:

> The smallest, most reliable combination of deterministic logic, specialized
> evidence, memory, model-assisted reasoning and external advice that produces
> a truthful, explainable, reproducible and safely governable Artemis judgment.

For Artemis identity, the governing principle is:

> Models may change. Providers may change. Agents may evolve. Artemis remains
> the canonical cognitive orchestrator, synthesizer and decision owner within
> its approved analytical authority boundary.


